# Clearance — .gitignore
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two things are ignored here, for two different reasons:
#   1. build output        — regenerable, and a committed binary is a lie.
#   2. compiled corpus     — a supply-chain control, not tidiness. See below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Go build output -------------------------------------------------------
/clearance
/clearance.exe
# NOTE: there is deliberately no rule for `corpus-build` here, and there used
# to be one. `/corpus-build` matched the corpus compiler's SOURCE DIRECTORY as
# well as any binary of that name, so git silently refused to track
# `corpus-build/main.go` and `corpus-build/sign.go` — the only program in the
# repository that can sign a corpus. A fresh clone could not compile the corpus
# at all: `make corpus`, `make corpus-sign` and `make corpus-verify` all failed
# with "directory not found", and the build guard that walks the script
# directories failed with it. The binary is covered by `/corpus-build.exe`
# below and by `*.exe` in the repo-root .gitignore; the source directory must
# never be ignored.
/corpus-build.exe
*.test
cover.out
/dist/

# --- Agent scratch space ---------------------------------------------------
# Local analysis artefacts. They live inside the module during a working
# session, so without a rule here a `git add -A` could commit them as if they
# were source. Named `.scratch/` precisely so nobody mistakes it for a feature.
/.scratch/

# --- Corpus build artefacts (NEVER commit) ---------------------------------
# The signed corpus bundle is a *release artefact*, not source. The source of
# truth is the YAML under corpus/**/*.yml.
#
# Committing the compiled bundle would (a) create a second source of truth that
# can drift from the YAML, and (b) defeat the offline-key control: the signature
# is produced by an Ed25519 key that never touches a build server or a
# contributor machine (ADR-003, INV-9). If the bundle were tracked, a pull
# request could modify the thing the binary trusts without ever touching the
# YAML a reviewer reads.
corpus.json
corpus.json.sig
corpus.version.json
# Guard against the old spelling of the signature file so a stale local file
# cannot be committed by accident. The frozen name is corpus.json.sig.
corpus.sig

# The staging directory `make corpus-dist` writes, which is what a release
# archive carries next to the binary. Its contents are already covered by the
# rules above; this names the directory so the intent is legible.
/corpus-dist/

# The OFFLINE signing private key. The PUBLIC half is tracked deliberately
# (corpus-public-key.hex) because it is not a secret and CI needs it to stamp
# the build. The private half is the only thing that can sign a corpus, so it
# must never be one `git add -A` away from being published.
corpus-signing.key
*.corpus-key

# --- OS / editor noise -----------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
